.TH CHMAP_DESTROY_WITH_DTOR 3 "2026-08-19" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_destroy_with_dtor \- destroy a hash map, running a destructor on every value first, without allocating memory
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

void chmap_destroy_with_dtor(chmap chm,
    void (*val_dtor)(const cmap_pair *val_pair, void *dtor_ctx),
    void *dtor_ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Destroys the map exactly as chmap_destroy(3) does, except that when
val_dtor is not NULL it is called once for each live entry, with that
entry's own value as a cmap_pair, right before the entry is freed.

The function allocates no memory of its own. It walks the map's internal
storage directly and calls the destructor inside that walk, while
walking with chashmap_begin_iter(3) and destroying afterwards does
allocate.

It is meant for a map whose values are owned pointers to bigger
structures that need their own recursive teardown, a dictionary of
dictionaries for example. You could also destroy such a map by walking
it with chashmap_begin_iter(3), running a value destructor on each entry
and destroying the empty map at the end, but that leaves a real gap
under memory pressure. The small internal allocation of
chashmap_begin_iter(3) can fail, and then there is no way left to reach
and free the values the map still owns. This function has no such gap,
since it allocates nothing.

The cmap_pair that val_dtor receives describes the stored value of the
entry. val_pair->ptr points into storage that the map is about to free,
val_pair->size is the size of the value, and the target is const. A
destructor reads val_pair->ptr and val_pair->size and frees whatever the
value itself owns, usually a pointer copied out of those bytes.
Assigning to val_pair->ptr or val_pair->size is a compile error.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm        Hash map to destroy
val_dtor   Destructor called once for each live value before its
           entry is freed, or NULL to destroy the map with no callback
           per value. Its val_pair parameter is a const cmap_pair *
dtor_ctx   Opaque pointer forwarded unchanged to every val_dtor call
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to call with a NULL chm (a no-op)
Does not set the chm handle to NULL; use chmap_destroy(3) when the
handle has to be cleared too
val_dtor must not change chm itself, because the map is in the middle
of its teardown for the whole call
The pair describes storage that the map is about to free, and the map
cannot own a pointer it did not allocate itself. That is why assigning
to either field of the pair is a compile error rather than just a rule
to remember
Allocates nothing of its own, so it cannot fail under any memory
condition that makes chashmap_begin_iter(3) fail
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_destroy (3),
.BR chmap_get_elem_ref (3),
.BR chashmap_begin_iter (3)
